"Beyond the circuit: How to Minimize Foreign Arithmetic in ZKP Circuits (https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/265)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/265\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 76\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2024/265\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Beyond the circuit: How to Minimize Foreign Arithmetic in ZKP Circuits】\nL32: \nL33: Michele Orrù, French National Centre for Scientific Research\nL34: \nL35: George Kadianakis, Ethereum Foundation\nL36: \nL37: Mary Maller, Ethereum Foundation, PQShield\nL38: \nL39: Greg Zaverucha, Microsoft Research\nL40: ##### Abstract\nL41: Zero-knowledge circuits are frequently required to prove gadgets that are not optimised for the constraint system in question. A particularly daunting task is to embed foreign arithmetic such as Boolean operations, field arithmetic, or public-key cryptography.\nL42: We construct techniques for offloading foreign arithmetic from a zero-knowledge circuit including: (i) equality of discrete logarithms across different groups; (ii) scalar multiplication without requiring elliptic curve operations; (iii) proving knowledge of an AES encryption. To achieve our goal, we employ techniques inherited from rejection sampling and lookup protocols. We implement and provide concrete benchmarks for our protocols.\nL43: ##### Metadata\nL44: Available format(s)\nL45:     【17†PDF】\nL46: Category\nL47:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL48: Publication info\nL49:     Preprint.\nL50: Keywords\nL51:     【19†zero-knowledge】【20†argument of knowledge】【21†discrete logarithm equality】【22†aes】\nL52: Contact author(s)\nL53:     m @ orru net\nL54: george kadianakis @ ethereum org\nL55: mary maller @ ethereum org\nL56: gregz @ microsoft com\nL57: History\nL58:     2024-02-19: approved\nL59:     2024-02-16: received\nL60:     【23†See all versions】\nL61: Short URL\nL62:     【24†https://ia.cr/2024/265†ia.cr】\nL63: License\nL64: \nL65: 【25†CC0†creativecommons.org】\nL66: BibTeX 【26†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL67: \nL68:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2024/265,\nL69:           author = {Michele Orrù and George Kadianakis and Mary Maller and Greg Zaverucha},\nL70:           title = {Beyond the circuit: How to Minimize Foreign Arithmetic in {ZKP} Circuits},\nL71:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2024/265},\nL72:           year = {2024},\nL73:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/265}\nL74:     }\nL75: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProof-Carrying Data via Holography Accumulation (https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/538)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/538\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 76\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2026/538\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Proof-Carrying Data via Holography Accumulation】\nL32: \nL33: Nikitas Paslis, Universitat Pompeu Fabra\nL34: \nL35: Carla Ràfols, Universitat Pompeu Fabra\nL36: \nL37: Alexandros Zacharakis, Hasso Plattner Institute, University of Potsdam\nL38: ##### Abstract\nL39: Succinct non-interactive arguments of knowledge (SNARKs) enable the verification of complex computations via short proofs. Recursive proof composition allows long-running or distributed computations to be verified incrementally, but existing approaches exhibit a fundamental trade-off.\nL40: Folding-based schemes achieve highly efficient recursion but require provers to maintain and communicate large private state, while stateless approaches such as full SNARK recursion and atomic accumulation incur higher prover costs due to the need to produce and verify a full SNARK proof at each step. We introduce holography accumulation, a framework for stateless recursive proving for SNARKs based on the lincheck or checkable subspace arguments.\nL41: These SNARKs admit a natural decomposition of verification into witness-dependent checks and public polynomial evaluations encoding the computation. We show that the latter, which we call holographic checks, can be accumulated efficiently across recursive steps. To formalize this idea, we introduce generalized bilinear forms (GBF), a linear-algebraic abstraction capturing the holographic verification procedures of several modern SNARKs.\nL42: Using this abstraction, we construct generic PCD schemes compatible with both univariate and multivariate polynomial commitment schemes, and present an efficient decider that collapses the accumulated checks to a single polynomial evaluation.\nL43: ##### Metadata\nL44: Available format(s)\nL45:     【17†PDF】\nL46: Category\nL47:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL48: Publication info\nL49:     Published elsewhere. Minor revision. SCN 2026\nL50: Keywords\nL51:     【19†SNARK】【20†accumulation】【21†folding】【22†recursive proof composition】【23†IVC】【24†PCD】\nL52: Contact author(s)\nL53:     nikitas paslis @ upf edu\nL54: carla rafols @ upf edu\nL55: alexandros zacharakis @ hpi de\nL56: History\nL57:     2026-06-08: last of 3 revisions\nL58:     2026-03-17: received\nL59:     【25†See all versions】\nL60: Short URL\nL61:     【26†https://ia.cr/2026/538†ia.cr】\nL62: License\nL63: 【27†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL64: BibTeX 【28†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL65: \nL66:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2026/538,\nL67:           author = {Nikitas Paslis and Carla Ràfols and Alexandros Zacharakis},\nL68:           title = {Proof-Carrying Data via Holography Accumulation},\nL69:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2026/538},\nL70:           year = {2026},\nL71:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/538}\nL72:     }\nL73: \nL74: \nL75: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - mmaker/tinybear · GitHub (https://github.com/mmaker/tinybear)\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/mmaker/tinybear\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 309\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:     