ork3 (3)】\nL137:   * 【70†Star25 (25)】You must be signed in to star a repository\nL138: \nL139: ## About\nL140: \nL141: No description, website, or topics provided.\nL142: ### Resources\nL143: \nL144: 【71†Readme】\nL145: \nL146: 【72†BSD-3-Clause license】\nL147: \nL148: 【73†Activity】\nL149: \nL150: ### Stars\nL151: \nL152: 25 stars\nL153: \nL154: ### Watchers\nL155: \nL156: 2 watching\nL157: \nL158: ### Forks\nL159: \nL160: 【74†3 forks】\nL161: \nL162: 【75†Report repository】\nL163: \nL164: main\nL165: \nL166: 【76†Branches】【77†Tags】\nL167: \nL168: [Input: Go to file]\nL169: \nL170: Go to file\nL171: \nL172: Code\nL173: \nL174: Open more actions menu\nL175: \nL176: ## Latest commit\nL177: \nL178: \nL179: \nL180: ## History\nL181: \nL182: 44 Commits\nL183: ## Folders and files\nL184: \nL185: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL186: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL187: 【78†benches】  | 【78†benches】  |    |\nL188: 【79†src】  | 【79†src】  |    |\nL189: 【80†.gitignore】  | 【80†.gitignore】  |    |\nL190: 【81†AUTHORS】  | 【81†AUTHORS】  |    |\nL191: 【82†Cargo.toml】  | 【82†Cargo.toml】  |    |\nL192: 【83†LICENSE】  | 【83†LICENSE】  |    |\nL193: 【84†README.md】  | 【84†README.md】  |    |\nL194: 【85†proof_size.py】  | 【85†proof_size.py】  |    |\nL195: ## Repository files navigation\nL196: \nL197:   *   * 【86†README】\nL198:   * 【86†BSD-3-Clause license】\nL199: \nL200: More items\nL201: # Tinybear (• ֎ •)\nL202: \nL203: WARNING: Proceed with caution! This code's not constant time and is probably broken in a dozen different ways. It has not been audited publicly, and the API will experience modifications.\nL204: \nL205: This is a gadget proof system for proving that\nL206: \nL207: $$\\text{ctx} = \\text{AES}(k, m).$$\nL208: \nL209: where $m$, the message, is committed as a Pedersen commitment $M$ and $k$, the key, is committed with the other round keys into a Pedersen commitment $K$.\nL210: Internally, the protocol uses 1 single invocation of a lookup protocol. More precisely, the AES circuit is translated into a lookup of:\nL211: \nL212:   * AES-128: 1808 elements in a table of 768 elements.\nL213:   * AES-256: 2576 elements in a table of 768 elements.\nL214: \nL215: we use log-derivates techniques from 【87†[Hab22]†eprint.iacr.org】, good old sumcheck, and and 𝛴-protocols to prove the polynomial relation.\nL216: ## Proof size\nL217: \nL218: Proof size: 80KB (>78.98KB are the 𝛴-protocols 💀).\nL219: ## Performances\nL220: \nL221: Proving time for a Mac M1 Pro.\nL222: \nL223:     `aes128/prove            time:   [28.198 ms 28.403 ms 28.644 ms]\nL224:     aes256/prove            time:   [33.040 ms 33.505 ms 34.060 ms]\nL225:     aes128/verify           time:   [12.334 ms 12.606 ms 12.937 ms]\nL226:     aes256/verify           time:   [12.966 ms 13.034 ms 13.112 ms]\nL227:     `\nL228: without multithreading:\nL229: \nL230:     `aes128/prove            time:   [65.047 ms 65.178 ms 65.318 ms]\nL231:     aes256/prove            time:   [69.770 ms 69.851 ms 69.938 ms]\nL232:     aes128/verify           time:   [36.984 ms 37.030 ms 37.083 ms]\nL233:     aes256/verify           time:   [44.802 ms 44.920 ms 45.060 ms]\nL234:     `\nL235: on battery and without multithreading:\nL236: \nL237:     `aes128/prove            time:   [84.010 ms 84.062 ms 84.118 ms]\nL238:     aes256/prove            time:   [91.787 ms 91.909 ms 92.042 ms]\nL239:     aes128/verify           time:   [48.744 ms 48.788 ms 48.834 ms]\nL240:     aes256/verify           time:   [58.989 ms 59.089 ms 59.203 ms]\nL241:     `\nL242: ## High-level overview\nL243: \nL244: AES can be implemented using only 3 operations:\nL245: \nL246:   * 4-bit XOR, a map $(\\mathbb{F}_2^4)^2 \\to (\\mathbb{F}_2^4)$\nL247:   * SBOX, a map $\\mathbb{F}_2^8 \\to \\mathbb{F}_2^8$\nL248:   * RJ2 (multiplication by Rijndael(2)), a map $\\mathbb{F}_2^8 \\to \\mathbb{F}_2^8$.\nL249: The prover commits to inputs $\\textsf{in}$ and outputs $\\textsf{out}$ for each function (the initial input, the keys, and the final output are part of the statement). The verifier sends a challege $c$. The prover shows that $\\textsf{in} + c \\cdot \\textsf{out}$ is contained in the table of all possible evaluations (in this case, made of $256\\cdot 3 = 768$ elements). This is a lookup protocol.\nL250: The lookup protocol allows us to check that a vector $\\vec f$ committed by the prover has all its elements contained in a vector $\\vec t$ (known by the verifier) if there exists $\\vec m$ such that the rational polynomial equation below is satisfied:\nL251: \nL252: $$ \\sum_i \\frac{m_i}{x + t_i} = \\sum_i \\frac{1}{x + f_i} $$\nL253: \nL254: (Informally, $\\vec m$ is the number of times each $t_i$ appears in $\\vec f$.) The above equation can be tested as follows:\nL255:   * the prover send a commitment to $\\vec m$,\nL256:   * the verifier send a challenge $c_l$ and computes $\\vec h = [\\frac{1}{c_l + t_i}]_i$\nL257:   * the prover sends a commitment to $\\vec g = [\\frac{1}{c_l + f_i}]_i$\nL258: \nL259: Then running an inner-product to prove that:\nL260: \nL261:   * $\\langle \\vec m, \\vec h\\rangle = \\langle \\vec g, \\vec 1\\rangle$\nL262:   * $\\vec g \\circ (\\vec f + c_l) = \\vec 1$\nL263: \nL264: We implement the inner-product using sumcheck and Schnorr proofs (oh yes).\nL265: ## Improvements\nL266: \nL267: A lot of techniques will improve the asymptotic and concrete efficiency of the protocol.\nL268: \nL269:   1. Fixed-base multiplication\nL270:   2. log-sized protocols (à-la-bulletproofs) for reducing the proof size.\nL271:   3. Lowering the sumcheck challenges to 16 bytes. This should maintain soundness guarantees in the generic group model.\nL272: \nL273: ## About\nL274: \nL275: No description, website, or topics provided.\nL276: \nL277: ### Resources\nL278: \nL279: 【71†Readme】\nL280: \nL281: 【72†BSD-3-Clause license】\nL282: \nL283: 【73†Activity】\nL284: \nL285: ### Stars\nL286: \nL287: 25 stars\nL288: \nL289: ### Watchers\nL290: \nL291: 2 watching\nL292: ### Forks\nL293: \nL294